THREE BUGS FOUND BY MEASURING RATHER THAN TRUSTING, all mine, all in this
directive:

1. The sampling gate had a 16 MiB budget and a 32-sample floor in tension.
   en_100 averages 549 KB per article, so the budget bought 31 samples -- one
   short -- and the dictionary was SILENTLY skipped. Caught because the v0.10
   pack came out byte-identical to the v0.9 one, which should be impossible;
   checking the codec histogram rather than accepting the size found it.
   Fixed by capping each sample at 64 KiB, which is what dict training wants
   anyway. For en_100 the buggy path reached the right answer by accident.

2. zim2wax trained a fresh dictionary for a new EDITION. Carry-forward was
   wired into open_append, which covers appending to one file -- but nobody
   appends to a Wikipedia pack, they convert next month's ZIM. Added
   --carry-dictionary-from. Measured on two real editions: carried forward
   reframes 0 of 15,589 unchanged entries; retrained reframes 14,216 and
   wastes 2,946,912 bytes, 24.2% of the update, on content that did not change.

3. The reader handed zstd the raw 110 KB dictionary on EVERY read, so every
   read re-digested it: 16.3us against v0.9's 12.7us, 28% slower -- the new
   codec would have been worse to read than the one it replaced. D04's
   codecbench missed this because it built its decompressor outside the timer.
   Preparing the dictionary once at open: 11.4us, now ~10% FASTER than v0.9.

Worth carrying forward: carry-forward costs ~12% at-rest size on this pair
(21.2 MB carried vs 19.0 MB retrained) in exchange for zero delta waste. Over
many editions the delta saving compounds and the size penalty does not, but a
deliberate periodic retrain is worth evaluating -- SPEC 5.9 already allows it
as a flagged event.

Converter scratch: proven against a real OOM kill at 128M. Exit 137, leaves
out.wax.partial, nothing at the output path.

